@startuml
title Realize geometry from fixed topology (priority-driven flood-fill onto macro-cells)

start
:Each free SeedGraphNode's position becomes\na growth seed; its CSP-assigned identity's\nsize range converts to a target cell count;
:Seed cell claimed for each patch\n(skipped if the seed spot itself turns out\nillegal - GapFiller's sweep below\nreclaims that territory from a neighbor);

while (any patch still below\nits target and has claimable frontier?) is (yes)
  :Pick the patch furthest below\nits target (priority);
  :Pop its highest-priority frontier cell\n(nearest-to-seed + deterministic jitter -\nsame ordering GrowthEngine uses today);
  if (still unclaimed, still legal,\nstill cohesive right now?) then (yes)
    :Claim it for this patch;
    :Push its legal, cohesive,\nstill-unclaimed neighbors\ninto this patch's frontier;
  else (no)
    :Discard - gone stale since it was\npushed (a competing patch claimed it,\nor a neighbor's claim since then\nmade it illegal/incohesive);
  endif
endwhile (no)

:Run GapFiller.fillGaps over whatever's\nstill unclaimed - the same always-\nterminating multi-source-BFS-plus-three-\ntier-resolver the flood-fill-first\npipeline already relies on, unchanged;

stop

note right
  Legality/cohesion are NOT fully pre-decided by
  the CSP stage - Delaunay edges approximate which
  seeds will actually end up touching in the final
  geometry, they don't guarantee it exactly. So
  every claim here is still checked live against
  the CURRENT shared assignment (same "recheck
  fresh, don't trust push-time state" principle
  GapFiller's own fill pass already uses) - this
  stage narrows how OFTEN a conflict can happen
  (CSP already resolved the common case), it
  doesn't eliminate the need for the check.
end note
@enduml
